- run([1, 2], 0)
- run([1], -1)
- run([], -1)
- run([5, 5, 5], -1)
- [run(list(range(5000)), 2500), run(list(range(5000)), -1), run([7] * 5000, 4999)]
fail[2]: The last node links back to the head, so the loop is the whole list.
fail[3]: One node whose `next` is None: no cycle.
fail[4]: An empty list: head is None, and there's no cycle.
fail[5]: Every value is 5, but the list ends: compare nodes with `is`, not values.
fail[6]: Lists of 5000 nodes, the last looping on itself. Keeping the nodes seen in a Python list is O(n²); Floyd's two pointers are O(n).
edges:
- an empty list :: len(values) == 0 :: run([], -1)
- no cycle :: len(values) > 0 and pos == -1 :: run([1, 2], -1)
- a cycle :: pos >= 0 :: run([1, 2], 1)
edge_count: 2
complexity: time=O(n) | space=O(1)
complexity_note: n is the number of nodes.
why_time: Before the pointers meet, `fast` runs off the end of a straight list, or catches `slow` within one lap of the loop: O(n) steps.
why_space: Two pointers, whatever the length.
hint: Start `slow` and `fast` at head. Loop while `fast is not None and fast.next is not None`.
hint: Inside: `slow = slow.next` and `fast = fast.next.next`; then, if `slow is fast`, return True.
hint: If the loop ends, `fast` reached the end of the list: return False.
right: If the list ends, the fast pointer finds the end; if it loops, the fast pointer gains one node a step on the slow one, so it catches up within one lap of the loop.
wrong: Move slow one node and fast two nodes at a time from the head; return True if they're ever the same node, and False when fast reaches None.

