- run([1], 0)
- run([1, 2, 3, 4, 5], 4)
- [run(list(range(6000)), 1500), run(list(range(4000)), -1), run([1] * 2000, 0)]
fail[2]: The last node links back to the head: both nodes are on the loop.
fail[3]: One node and no loop: 0.
fail[4]: One node that links to itself: a loop of 1.
fail[5]: Only the last node is on the loop, linking to itself.
fail[6]: Loops of 4500 and 2000 nodes, and a list of 4000 that ends. Keeping the nodes seen in a Python list is O(n²).
edges:
- an empty list :: len(values) == 0 :: run([], -1)
- no loop :: len(values) > 0 and pos == -1 :: run([1], -1)
- a loop :: pos >= 0 :: run([1], 0)
edge_count: 2
complexity: time=O(n) | space=O(1)
complexity_note: n is the number of nodes.
why_time: Floyd's pointers meet within O(n) steps, and the walk round the loop is at most n more.
why_space: A few pointers and a count.
hint: Run Floyd's loop. When `slow is fast`, keep `meet = slow` and break; if the loop ends without a meeting, return 0.
hint: Start `length = 1` and `node = meet.next`. While `node is not meet`, add 1 to length and step on.
right: The meeting node is on the loop, so a walk from it comes back to it after exactly one lap, which counts the loop's nodes with O(1) extra space.
wrong: Use Floyd's slow and fast pointers to find a node on the loop (0 if fast reaches None), then walk round once from it, counting nodes until you're back.

=== task: code medium
title: Minimum Swaps to Group All 1's Together II
leetcode: 2134 | Minimum Swaps to Group All 1's Together II | minimum-swaps-to-group-all-1s-together-ii
