fail[3]: 5, 4, 6 rotate two places to 6, 5, 4, and −9 stays where it is.
fail[4]: No non-negative numbers: nothing moves, and there's no circle to build.
fail[5]: k is 0, so nothing moves. 0 counts as non-negative.
fail[6]: 15,000 numbers and k = 99,999.
edges:
- no non-negative numbers :: all(x < 0 for x in nums) :: run([-1], 1)
- k a multiple of the count :: any(x >= 0 for x in nums) and k % sum(1 for x in nums if x >= 0) == 0 :: run([1], 1)
- a real rotation :: any(x >= 0 for x in nums) and k % sum(1 for x in nums if x >= 0) != 0 :: run([1, 2], 1)
edge_count: 2
complexity: time=O(n) | space=O(n)
complexity_note: n is the length of nums.
why_time: Building the circle and filling in the answer are a pass each, and the tail moves fewer than m times.
why_space: The circle holds up to n nodes, and the answer is a new list of n numbers.
hint: Collect the non-negative numbers in kept; if kept is empty, return a copy of nums.
hint: `tail = from_list(kept)`, then `tail = tail.next`, `k % len(kept)` times.
hint: `node = tail.next`. For each index i where `result[i] >= 0`: `result[i] = node.val`, then `node = node.next`.
right: Rotating left by one place is one move of the tail, and m moves come back to the start, so k % m moves give the rotation, however big k is.
wrong: Put the non-negative numbers in a circle, move its tail k % m times, then walk round from the first node, writing the values back into the non-negative places in order.

=== task: code medium
